#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xgjobs -- the jobs board's command-line interface: agents express intent
(start a job, log an update, mark it done), this tool owns every mechanical
detail (timestamps, locking, format, rebuilding). Hand-editing jobs/*.md
directly is DEPRECATED for agents as of 2026-08-09 -- it stays as a
documented emergency path (recovering a file this tool refuses to touch,
say), not the normal way to update the board. See tools/build_jobs.py's
docstring for the underlying jobs/*.md field contract if you ever do need
to hand-edit.

Why this exists: hand-typed timestamps drifted against the NFS mount's
clock skew, and a board-registration edit once put a `needs:` line in the
middle of an append-only `log:` block, silently truncating the log on every
render. This tool removes both failure classes structurally: one time
source (this workstation's local clock, never a file mtime or a hand-typed
guess), and a fixed field layout that only this tool writes.

Verbs:
    xgjobs start <slug> --title T --motivation M --executor E
        (--page NAME | --no-page REASON) [--log SENTENCE]
        Create jobs/<slug>.md. Refuses if the file already exists -- use
        log/done/frozen/reopen to update an existing job.
    xgjobs log <slug> "sentence"
        Append one dated log line to an existing job.
    xgjobs done <slug> --outcome "sentence"
        Set status=done; the outcome is also appended as the log's final
        line (it renders distinctly there, tagged "outcome").
    xgjobs frozen <slug> [--reason "sentence"]
        Set status=frozen, with a log line (a bare "Frozen." if no reason
        given).
    xgjobs reopen <slug> "why"
        Set status=ongoing again, with a log line explaining why.
    xgjobs flag <slug> --ask "what to open and judge"
        Set needs: evaluation -- MASTER-ONLY BY CONVENTION (not enforced by
        this tool, there is no identity system to enforce it against): an
        executing agent reports done with an outcome, it does not flag its
        own work for the owner's review. Pins the row to the top of the
        board with a violet "for your review" chip until unflagged. --ask
        is REQUIRED: a bare flag pins the row but gives the owner no hint
        what to open or judge, which is exactly the failure this verb
        exists to prevent -- omitting it is an error, not a shortcut.
    xgjobs unflag <slug>
        Clear needs: evaluation (the master, after the owner's verdict).

Every verb: reads the current file (if any) under an flock'd per-slug lock
so two concurrent calls on the same slug never interleave (see locked());
validates every user-supplied sentence against the writing standard BEFORE
touching anything (see lint() -- hard rules only: no em dash, none of the
banned words, no #<digits> task-id reference; it does not judge prose
quality); writes the result to a temp file, re-parses THAT with
inventory_jobs.parse() (the exact function the board renders with) to
confirm it round-trips intact, and only then atomically renames it into
place (see safe_write()) -- a validation failure leaves the real file
completely untouched. Rebuilds the live board's jobs tab as its last step
(tools/build_jobs.py under .venv_console, the same thin driver a human
would run by hand), so publishing is never a separate step.

AUTHORSHIP (2026-08-09, owner-directed: a first-person log line once read as
if it might be the owner's own words, when it was the executing agent's
self-assessment -- see inventory_jobs.py's LOG-LINE AUTHORSHIP section for
the render side). Every verb that appends a log line stamps WHO is speaking,
not just when: `--as NAME` if given, else the XGJOBS_ACTOR environment
variable if set, else a per-verb default -- "executor" for
start/log/done/frozen/reopen (the normal progress-report verbs, run by the
agent doing the work), "master" for flag/unflag (verbs that by convention
only the master runs, since they touch the master-owned needs: field). The
board renders the stamp as a small quiet label before the sentence; [owner]
alone reads with a touch more weight since owner words are the record of
verdicts, not progress notes. Every log line you write through this tool
states a fact in the SPEAKER's own voice ("self-check found: two things
that bother me about the layout"), never in a voice that could be misread
as someone else's, especially not the owner's -- that confusion is exactly
what this feature exists to prevent.

Examples (one per verb):
    tools/xgjobs start render_doc --executor render-doc \\
        --title "Name and document the project's rendering setups" \\
        --motivation "Figures use several lighting setups and nobody can tell which one a figure used." \\
        --no-page "not started yet"
    tools/xgjobs log render_doc "Read every render script and cross-checked each parameter against a real published render."
    tools/xgjobs done render_doc --outcome "RENDERING.md and a workspace page cover all five setups with verified parameters."
    tools/xgjobs frozen render_doc --reason "Blocked on a render farm allocation."
    tools/xgjobs reopen render_doc "The allocation came through."
    tools/xgjobs flag render_doc --ask "Open the page and check the comparison figure reads correctly."
    tools/xgjobs unflag render_doc
    tools/xgjobs log render_doc "Self-check found: the comparison figure's caption is missing units." --as jobs-redesign
"""
import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime
import fcntl
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys

REPO = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
JOBS = REPO / "jobs"
LOCK_DIR = JOBS / ".locks"
CONSOLE_PYTHON = REPO / ".venv_console" / "bin" / "python"
BUILD_JOBS = REPO / "tools" / "build_jobs.py"

sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / "tools"))
import inventory_jobs as ij  # noqa: E402  -- reuse the SAME parser the board renders with

TIME_FMT = "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"
# canonical field order; "needs" (optional, omitted when unset) and
# "motivation" are appended after this run, then log:/outcome -- see
# serialize(). Matches every hand-migrated jobs/*.md file already on disk.
FIELD_ORDER = ["title", "executor", "status", "started", "updated", "slurm", "link", "page"]

BANNED_WORDS = ["corpus", "audit", "contact sheet"]
TASK_ID_RE = re.compile(r"#\d+")
EM_DASH = "—"
SLUG_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*$")


class LintError(Exception):
    pass


def lint(text, field):
    """Hard rules only, per the brief -- no prose-quality judgment, just
    the three things that have actually broken the board's writing
    standard: an em dash, a banned word, or a bare task-id reference that
    means nothing to the owner reading the board."""
    errors = []
    if EM_DASH in text:
        errors.append(f'{field}: contains an em dash. Rewrite with a comma, '
                      f'period, or parentheses instead.')
    lowered = text.lower()
    for w in BANNED_WORDS:
        if w in lowered:
            errors.append(f'{field}: contains the banned word "{w}". Use plain language instead.')
    m = TASK_ID_RE.search(text)
    if m:
        errors.append(f'{field}: contains a task-id reference ("{m.group(0)}"). '
                      f'Describe what happened in plain words, not an internal tracking id.')
    if errors:
        raise LintError("\n".join(errors))


def now():
    return datetime.datetime.now().strftime(TIME_FMT)


def resolve_actor(args, default):
    """Every log-appending verb stamps WHO said a line, not just when (see
    inventory_jobs.py's LOG-LINE AUTHORSHIP): --as NAME wins if given, else
    the XGJOBS_ACTOR environment variable, else `default` (the caller
    passes "executor" for the normal progress-report verbs, "master" for
    flag/unflag -- verbs that by convention only the master runs)."""
    return getattr(args, "as_", None) or os.environ.get("XGJOBS_ACTOR") or default


@contextlib.contextmanager
def locked(slug):
    """One flock'd lockfile per slug (not the job file itself, so `start`
    can lock a slug before its .md exists). Held across the whole
    read-modify-write cycle of every verb, so two concurrent calls on the
    SAME slug serialize instead of interleaving; different slugs never
    contend."""
    LOCK_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    f = open(LOCK_DIR / f"{slug}.lock", "a+")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(f.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        yield
    finally:
        fcntl.flock(f.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        f.close()


def job_path(slug):
    if not SLUG_RE.match(slug):
        sys.exit(f'error: "{slug}" is not a valid slug (lowercase letters, digits, '
                 f'_ and - only, must start with a letter or digit)')
    return JOBS / f"{slug}.md"


def require_exists(slug):
    path = job_path(slug)
    if not path.exists():
        sys.exit(f'error: jobs/{slug}.md does not exist. Use "xgjobs start {slug} ..." first.')
    return path


def serialize(e):
    lines = [f"{key}: {e.get(key) or ''}" for key in FIELD_ORDER]
    if e.get("needs"):
        lines.append(f"needs: {e['needs']}")
    lines.append(f"motivation: {e.get('motivation') or ''}")
    lines.append("log:")
    for ts, author, text in e["log"]:
        # author is None for a legacy line read back unchanged (never add a
        # label to a line that didn't have one); a real author renders as
        # the "[name]" token inventory_jobs.py's LOG_LINE_RE expects.
        lines.append(f"- {ts} [{author}] {text}" if author else f"- {ts} {text}")
    lines.append(f"outcome: {e.get('outcome') or ''}")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len):
    """Write to a temp file, re-parse it with inventory_jobs.parse() (the
    exact function the board renders with, not a reimplementation), and
    confirm the round trip is intact (right number of log lines, title and
    status survived) before atomically replacing the real file. A
    validation failure leaves the real file, if any, completely untouched
    -- this tool never writes a file the board can't read correctly."""
    text = serialize(e)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".md.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(text)
    reparsed = ij.parse(tmp)
    ok = (len(reparsed["log"]) == expected_log_len
          and reparsed.get("title") == (e.get("title") or "")
          and reparsed.get("status") == (e.get("status") or "")
          and reparsed.get("needs", "") == (e.get("needs") or ""))
    if not ok:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        sys.exit("error: the file this command produced did not round-trip through the "
                 "board's own parser -- refusing to write it. This is a bug in xgjobs, "
                 "not your input; the real file was not touched.")
    tmp.replace(path)


def rebuild_board():
    result = subprocess.run([str(CONSOLE_PYTHON), str(BUILD_JOBS)],
                            capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        print(f"warning: board rebuild failed (the job file itself was saved fine):\n"
             f"{result.stderr}", file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        print(result.stdout.strip() or "board rebuilt", file=sys.stderr)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- verbs ----
def cmd_start(args):
    path = job_path(args.slug)
    if bool(args.page) == bool(args.no_page):
        sys.exit('error: pass exactly one of --page NAME or --no-page REASON.')
    lint(args.title, "title")
    lint(args.motivation, "motivation")
    first_log = args.log or "Job started."
    lint(first_log, "log")
    page_value = args.page
    if args.no_page:
        lint(args.no_page, "--no-page reason")
        page_value = f"none ({args.no_page})"
    with locked(args.slug):
        if path.exists():
            sys.exit(f'error: jobs/{args.slug}.md already exists. "start" never overwrites; '
                     f'use log/done/frozen/reopen to update it.')
        ts = now()
        author = resolve_actor(args, "executor")
        e = {"title": args.title, "executor": args.executor, "status": "ongoing",
             "started": ts, "updated": ts, "slurm": "", "link": "", "page": page_value,
             "motivation": args.motivation, "log": [(ts, author, first_log)], "outcome": ""}
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=1)
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md created", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_log(args):
    lint(args.sentence, "log")
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        e["log"].append((ts, resolve_actor(args, "executor"), args.sentence))
        e["updated"] = ts
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: logged", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_done(args):
    lint(args.outcome, "outcome")
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        e["status"] = "done"
        e["outcome"] = args.outcome
        e["updated"] = ts
        e["log"].append((ts, resolve_actor(args, "executor"), args.outcome))
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: done", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_frozen(args):
    line = f"Frozen: {args.reason}" if args.reason else "Frozen."
    lint(line, "log")
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        e["status"] = "frozen"
        e["updated"] = ts
        e["log"].append((ts, resolve_actor(args, "executor"), line))
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: frozen", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_reopen(args):
    lint(args.why, "log")
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        e["status"] = "ongoing"
        e["updated"] = ts
        e["log"].append((ts, resolve_actor(args, "executor"), args.why))
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: reopened", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_flag(args):
    # --ask is REQUIRED (owner feedback 2026-08-09): a bare flag pins the
    # row but gives the owner no hint what to open or judge, which is
    # exactly the failure this verb exists to prevent. Checked here (not
    # via argparse required=True) so the error explains WHY, not just that
    # an argument is missing.
    if not args.ask:
        sys.exit('error: --ask is required. A bare review flag pins the row but gives the '
                 'owner no hint what to open or judge; say what to check in one sentence, '
                 'e.g. --ask "Open the new page and check figure 3 reads correctly."')
    lint(args.ask, "--ask")
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        author = resolve_actor(args, "master")
        e["needs"] = f"evaluation: {args.ask}"
        e["updated"] = ts
        # The ask goes INTO the log (owner-ratified): the needs: banner is
        # current state and vanishes when cleared; this line is the permanent
        # record. The renderer styles master lines with this prefix violet.
        e["log"].append((ts, author, f"For your review: {args.ask}"))
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: flagged for review", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def cmd_unflag(args):
    path = require_exists(args.slug)
    with locked(args.slug):
        e = ij.parse(path)
        ts = now()
        author = resolve_actor(args, "master")
        e["needs"] = ""
        e["updated"] = ts
        e["log"].append((ts, author, "Review complete; flag cleared."))
        safe_write(path, e, expected_log_len=len(e["log"]))
    print(f"jobs/{args.slug}.md: unflagged", file=sys.stderr)
    rebuild_board()


def add_as_arg(p, default_hint):
    """--as is on every verb that appends a log line: explicit attribution
    beats the XGJOBS_ACTOR env var beats this verb's own default (see
    resolve_actor()). Shared here so the seven subparsers describe it
    identically instead of drifting."""
    p.add_argument("--as", dest="as_", metavar="NAME",
                   help=f'who is speaking in this line (default: {default_hint}, or the '
                        f'XGJOBS_ACTOR environment variable if set) -- "executor", "master", '
                        f'"owner", or an agent\'s actual name all work, rendered exactly as given')


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="xgjobs", description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)

    p = sub.add_parser("start", help="create a new job entry")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    p.add_argument("--title", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--motivation", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--executor", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--page", help="the canonical pages.json name this job's results page will use")
    p.add_argument("--no-page", metavar="REASON", help="this job legitimately produces no page")
    p.add_argument("--log", help='first log line (default: "Job started.")')
    add_as_arg(p, '"executor"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_start)

    p = sub.add_parser("log", help="append one dated update line")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    p.add_argument("sentence")
    add_as_arg(p, '"executor"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_log)

    p = sub.add_parser("done", help="mark the job done with an outcome")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    p.add_argument("--outcome", required=True)
    add_as_arg(p, '"executor"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_done)

    p = sub.add_parser("frozen", help="mark the job frozen")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    p.add_argument("--reason")
    add_as_arg(p, '"executor"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_frozen)

    p = sub.add_parser("reopen", help="set an already done/frozen job back to ongoing")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    p.add_argument("why")
    add_as_arg(p, '"executor"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_reopen)

    p = sub.add_parser("flag", help="set needs: evaluation (MASTER-ONLY by convention)")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    # NOT argparse required=True on purpose: cmd_flag() checks this itself
    # so the error explains WHY --ask is required, not just that it's
    # missing (argparse's own "required" error can't carry that context).
    p.add_argument("--ask", help='REQUIRED: what the owner should open and '
                   'judge, one sentence, e.g. "Open the new page and check figure 3 reads '
                   'correctly." A bare flag with no ask gives the owner nothing to act on.')
    add_as_arg(p, '"master"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_flag)

    p = sub.add_parser("unflag", help="clear needs: evaluation (MASTER-ONLY by convention)")
    p.add_argument("slug")
    add_as_arg(p, '"master"')
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_unflag)

    args = ap.parse_args()
    try:
        args.func(args)
    except LintError as exc:
        sys.exit(f"error: writing standard violation(s):\n{exc}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
